# Line endings.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites checked-out files to CRLF. Prettier
# defaults to `endOfLine: "lf"`, so every file Git touched started failing `prettier --check`
# locally - a false failure that appears out of nowhere after a commit or a branch switch and
# has nothing to do with the code. CI never saw it (it runs on Linux, where autocrlf is off),
# which makes it purely a local time-waster.
#
# Normalising to LF in the working tree removes the disagreement at the source rather than
# re-running `prettier --write` every time Git converts something back.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exception: PowerShell scripts stay CRLF. Windows PowerShell 5.1 is the runtime here (see
# docs/DEVELOPMENT-SETUP.md), and CLAUDE.md already records that it is particular about how
# these files are encoded - CRLF is the native, known-good form for them, and nothing in the
# toolchain checks their formatting.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files Git must never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.gz binary
